---
Part XI, Chapter 3: Releasing on a push
Package: .github/workflows/
Language: YAML
---

# Releasing on a push

When the gate passes on the default branch, this workflow publishes the npm packages, attaches the standalone executables to a GitHub release, and brings the Zed and Gram extension's mirror up to date. It does all three on every push, so npm, the releases and the mirror always follow the default branch. Running on every push is only safe because each job is idempotent: the release tool publishes only versions npm is missing, the executables go only to a release that lacks them, and the mirror receives only commits it lacks. The website's image ships from the gate's own workflow instead, and only when the website changed.

The workflow triggers on the gate's completion rather than on the push directly, so it runs only after the checks have passed. Its job checks that the gate succeeded, on the default branch, before it does anything, and checks out the exact commit the gate validated rather than whatever the default branch points at now.

## The trigger

The workflow starts when the CI workflow finishes. It publishes to npm through Trusted Publishing, which needs the run to prove who it is, so it grants the token an identity and nothing to write. The one job that writes to the repository, by creating a release, asks for that right itself.

=>

name: Release

on:
  workflow_run:
    workflows: [CI]
    types: [completed]

permissions:
  contents: read
  id-token: write

## Publishing to npm

The npm job publishes through Trusted Publishing, so it stores no token. GitHub mints a short-lived identity for the job — this is what `id-token: write` grants — and npm exchanges it for publish rights, having been told on its side to trust this repository's `release.yaml` workflow. The job upgrades npm to a version that speaks the exchange, then runs the release tool, which publishes each package npm is missing and skips the rest, so a push that changes no version publishes nothing.

=>

jobs:
  npm:
    if: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'success' && github.event.workflow_run.head_branch == 'main' }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.head_sha }}
      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
        with:
          bun-version: 1.2.20
      - run: npm install --global npm@latest
      - run: bun install --frozen-lockfile
      - name: Publish
        run: bun scripts/release.ts

## Attaching the executables to a release

The standalone executable ships as a file on a GitHub release, one release for each version, tagged with that version and a `v` in front. This job builds the files and attaches them. It runs after the npm job, because an executable installs its language services from npm at its own version, and those must be published before anyone can download the executable.

The job is idempotent like the others. It reads the version from the CLI's manifest and asks GitHub whether that version's release already carries `SHA256SUMS`, the list of checksums. The job attaches the list last, so a release that has it has every file, and the job then stops there. A release that a failed run left half-filled lacks the list, so the next run fills it in.

=>

  binaries:
    needs: npm
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    permissions:
      contents: write
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.head_sha }}
      - uses: oven-sh/setup-bun@v2
        with:
          bun-version: 1.2.20
      - name: The release this version needs
        id: release
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
        run: |
          tag="v$(jq -r .version packages/loom/package.json)"
          echo "tag=$tag" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          if gh release view "$tag" --json assets --jq '.assets[].name' 2>/dev/null | grep -qx SHA256SUMS; then
            echo "done=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"
          fi

~

Bun compiles all five executables on one Linux runner. Each `--target` names a platform, and Bun fetches that platform's runtime to build into the file. The job builds the bundle once, compiles it five times, and names each file for its platform alone, with no version in the name, so whoever downloads one builds its name from the platform: `loom-darwin-arm64`, and `loom-windows-x64.exe`, since Bun gives the Windows file its `.exe`.

An executable carries a whole runtime, and gzip shrinks it to about a third, so the release ships each one gzipped: `loom-darwin-arm64.gz`, `loom-windows-x64.exe.gz`. Each holds the one file, and the install script, `loom upgrade` and Zed's extension all unpack it after download. The job copies the install script beside them, uncompressed, since `sh` reads it straight from the download. `sha256sum` then writes the checksum of every file the release carries into `SHA256SUMS`, so each file is checked as it is downloaded.

=>

      - name: An executable for each platform
        if: steps.release.outputs.done != 'true'
        run: |
          bun install --frozen-lockfile
          bun run --filter @athrio/loom build
          mkdir release
          for target in darwin-arm64 darwin-x64 linux-x64 linux-arm64 windows-x64; do
            bun build packages/loom/dist/main.js --compile --target "bun-$target" --outfile "release/loom-$target"
          done
          gzip -9 release/loom-*
          cp scripts/install.sh release/install.sh
          (cd release && sha256sum loom-*.gz install.sh > SHA256SUMS)

~

The release is created at the commit the gate validated, which also creates its tag, unless an earlier run already created it. It is a full release, never a prerelease, because GitHub's address for the latest release and Zed's search for it both pass over prereleases. The executables and the install script go up first and the list of checksums last. `--clobber` replaces a file an earlier run left half-uploaded.

The macOS executables carry no signature from Apple and have not been notarised. A file `curl` downloads carries no quarantine flag, so macOS runs it, and that is how the install script and `loom upgrade` fetch it. A file downloaded through a browser carries the flag, and macOS refuses to open it until its owner allows it. Signing and notarising them is work for a later release.

=>

      - name: Attach them to the release
        if: steps.release.outputs.done != 'true'
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
          TAG: ${{ steps.release.outputs.tag }}
          COMMIT: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.head_sha }}
        run: |
          if ! gh release view "$TAG" > /dev/null 2>&1; then
            gh release create "$TAG" --target "$COMMIT" --title "Loom ${TAG#v}" \
              --notes "Install the standalone executable with \`curl -fsSL https://github.com/athrio-com/loom/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh\`, or with npm as \`@athrio/loom\`. The macOS executables are not signed or notarised yet."
          fi
          gh release upload "$TAG" release/loom-*.gz release/install.sh --clobber
          gh release upload "$TAG" release/SHA256SUMS --clobber

## Mirroring the Zed extension

The extension for Zed and Gram lives in this repository, in `packages/tree-sitter-loom/editors/zed`. Gram installs an extension from a repository URL and reads its manifest from the repository's root, so the folder is mirrored to a repository of its own, `athrio-com/loom-zed`, whose root is the extension. This job keeps that mirror in step with the default branch, so nobody edits or pushes it by hand.

`git subtree split` builds the mirror's history from the folder's: one commit for each commit that changed the folder, holding the folder's files at the root. The split is deterministic. The same history gives the same commits, and a later split extends an earlier one, so the push to the mirror is a fast-forward, and a push that changed nothing in the folder sends nothing. The split reads the whole history, so the checkout fetches all of it.

The job writes to the mirror with a deploy key, an SSH key that can push to `athrio-com/loom-zed` and to no other repository. Its private half is the `LOOM_ZED_DEPLOY_KEY` secret of this repository, and the job hands it to Git for the one push.

=>

  zed:
    if: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'success' && github.event.workflow_run.head_branch == 'main' }}
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v5
        with:
          ref: ${{ github.event.workflow_run.head_sha }}
          fetch-depth: 0
      - name: Mirror the extension
        env:
          DEPLOY_KEY: ${{ secrets.LOOM_ZED_DEPLOY_KEY }}
        run: |
          key="$RUNNER_TEMP/loom-zed-key"
          printf '%s\n' "$DEPLOY_KEY" > "$key"
          chmod 600 "$key"
          split=$(git subtree split --prefix=packages/tree-sitter-loom/editors/zed HEAD)
          GIT_SSH_COMMAND="ssh -i $key -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new" \
            git push git@github.com:athrio-com/loom-zed.git "$split:refs/heads/main"

